Methodology

Step 1: Setting Up the MATLAB Simulation
The initial conditions and properties of the spacecraft are defined at the start in the MATLAB script.

Gravitational Parameter (mu): \(3.986 \times 10^{14} \, \text{m}^3/\text{s}^2\)
Earth’s Radius (Re): \(6,378 \, \text{km}\)
Spacecraft Mass: \(3 \, \text{kg}\)
Drag Coefficient (Cd): \(2.2\)
Cross-sectional Area for Drag: \(0.01281 \, \text{m}^2\)

The satellite’s initial position and velocity are specified in meters and meters per second:

Initial Position (X, Y, Z): \((4.211560342267748 \times 10^{-13}, 6878 \, \text{km}, 0)\)
Initial Velocity (VX, VY, VZ): \((0.9541223000883489, -5.842314103991534 \times 10^{-17}, 7.552655699128404)\)

There are forces in a simulation such as:

The gravitational force is computed according to Newton’s law of gravitation.
The atmospheric drag is modeled based on altitude, velocity, and density of the atmosphere. The density shows a pattern of exponential decay in terms of a standard reference value at an altitude of 150 km and the scale height of 60 km.

Here’s the code
function orbital_decay
    %% Constants
    mu = 3.986e14;  % Earth's gravitational parameter [m^3/s^2]
    Re = 6378e3;    % Earth's radius [m]

    %% Spacecraft Properties
    m  = 3;         % mass [kg]
    Cd = 2.2;       % drag coefficient
    A  = 0.01281;   % cross-sectional area [m^2]

    %% Initial Conditions
    x0  = 4.211560342267748e-13 * 1000;
    y0  = 6878e3;
    z0  = 0;
    vx0 = 0.9541223000883489 * 1000;
    vy0 = -5.842314103991534e-17 * 1000;
    vz0 = 7.552655699128404 * 1000;

    state0 = [x0; y0; z0; vx0; vy0; vz0];

    %% Simulation Parameters
    % approx. 10 years
    tspan = [0 3.15e8];  

    % Stop when altitude &lt;= 120 km
    options = odeset('Events', @(t, state) reentryEvent(t, state, Re), ...
                     'RelTol',1e-7, 'AbsTol',1e-9);

    %% Propagate
    [t, state, te, stateEvent] = ode45(@(t, state) odeEquations(t, state, mu, m, Cd, A, Re), tspan, state0, options);

    %% Check if event triggered
    if isempty(stateEvent)
        disp('Reentry altitude was not reached within the simulation time.');
    else
        r_event = norm(stateEvent(1:3));
        alt_event = r_event - Re;
        years = te/3.154e7;  % seconds to years

        fprintf('Reentry reached at t = %.2f days (%.2f years)\n', te/86400, years);
        fprintf('Reentry altitude = %.2f meters\n', alt_event);
    end

    %% Plot the 3D orbit
    figure;
    plot3(state(:,1), state(:,2), state(:,3), 'b-', 'LineWidth', 1.5); 
    hold on;
    if ~isempty(stateEvent)
        plot3(stateEvent(1), stateEvent(2), stateEvent(3), 'ro', 'MarkerSize', 8, 'LineWidth', 2);
        legend('Trajectory','Reentry Point');
    else
        legend('Trajectory');
    end
    xlabel('x [m]'); ylabel('y [m]'); zlabel('z [m]');
    title('Spacecraft Deorbiting Under Drag');
    grid on; axis equal;

    %% Plot altitude vs time
    alt = sqrt(state(:,1).^2 + state(:,2).^2 + state(:,3).^2) - Re;
    figure;
    plot(t/86400, alt/1000, 'LineWidth',1.5);
    xlabel('Time [days]');
    ylabel('Altitude [km]');
    title('Altitude Decay Over Time');
    grid on;
end

%% ODE function
function dstatedt = odeEquations(~, state, mu, m, Cd, A, Re)
    r_vec = state(1:3);
    v_vec = state(4:6);
    r = norm(r_vec);
    v = norm(v_vec);

    % Gravitational accel
    a_grav = -mu / r^3 * r_vec;

    % Altitude
    alt = r - Re;

    % Approximating GMAT's MSISE90 with F10.7=150
    rho0 = 2.8e-10;   % [kg/m^3] at 150 km, 2e-10
    H    = 6e4;     % scale height [m]
    rho = rho0 * exp(-(alt - 150e3)/H);

    % Drag accel
    a_drag = -0.5 * Cd * A / m * rho * v * v_vec;

    dstatedt = [v_vec; a_grav + a_drag];
end

%% Event function
function [value, isterminal, direction] = reentryEvent(~, state, Re)
    r = norm(state(1:3));
    alt = r - Re;
    value = alt - 120000;  
    isterminal = 1;       
    direction = -1;       
end
The propagation is achieved using the MATLAB ‘ode45’ solver, which solves the equations of motion over time. This simulation is continued until the satellite reaches the point of re-entry above the earth, namely 120 km of altitude, after which an event function is triggered to stop the propagation.


Step 2: Generating and Analyzing Data
Once the simulation is completed, some result values are returned by the script, which include:

Re-entry Time: The time (in days and years) when the satellite reaches the re-entry altitude.
Re-entry Altitude: The altitude at which re-entry occurs.
Trajectory Visualization: A 3D plot of the satellite’s path around Earth with the re-entry point-visible.
Altitude Decay Profile: The time-altitude plot illustrating how altitude decreases over time for a satellite.

